## 1. The Big Idea

_One sentence: **Skin is a collagen-scaffold wall weathered mostly by one force (UV, ~80%), and it is defended outside-in by an assembly whose layer thicknesses are the leverage proportions — a thick cheap protection layer carrying ~90% of the result, a thin expensive actives membrane on top, periodic repair, and a sliver of enhanced tier — so building it in the wrong order means paying to refinish a wall the weather is still tearing down.** The poster must make the reader feel, instantly, the proportion (the cheap outer layer is the load-bearing one), the direction (the dominant assault comes from outside, which is why outside-in is the only sane order), and the futility of the reversed build (expensive interior finish under a missing roof)._

## 2. Visual Metaphor & Structure — the hero object

**An architect's WALL SECTION of skin, cut vertically and read OUTSIDE-IN, where the dermis is a collagen-scaffold wall sunk into the body, four aging forces bear down on it as labeled weather arrows of wildly unequal magnitude, and the protocol is drawn as an outside-in protective assembly whose layer thicknesses ARE the leverage proportions.** This is the anchor; nothing replaces it. The skin is drawn as what the article literally calls it — *the largest organ, a barrier, a substrate, a load-bearing structure* — so "section," "scaffold," "load-bearing step," "chronic substrate" render as architecture without forcing.

Read **outside-in, top/left → in** (this matters — the eye must travel the direction of attack and the order of the build):

- **THE WEATHER (outside, top) — the four ranked forces.** Four labeled arrows strike the wall from outside, and **their sizes are the argument**:
  - `PHOTOAGING · UV · ~80%` — a single **dominant** arrow, by far the largest, in alarm red, driving down/in onto the outer face. Sub-tag `UVA/UVB → MMPs cut the collagen scaffold.`
  - `INTRINSIC · TIME` — a thin steady arrow, `fibroblasts slow · collagen −1%/yr.`
  - `GLYCATION · SUGAR` — a medium arrow angled in from the *inside/diet* side, `AGEs cross-link & stiffen the scaffold.`
  - `HORMONAL` — a small arrow, `thickness · sebum · healing.`
  The four-to-one size ratio must be unmistakable at a glance: **one force dwarfs the other three combined.** That single visual fact justifies the entire outside-in order.

- **THE PROTECTIVE ASSEMBLY (the layers, outermost-first) — thickness = leverage.** Against the weather, the wall is clad in an outside-in assembly. **Each layer's drawn thickness equals its share of the result** — proportion *is* the message:
  - **Layer 1 — THE 90% (thickest, outermost, load-bearing slab):** `PROTECT` — SPF + UV avoidance, sleep, diet, no smoking. Drawn as the dominant outer cladding intercepting the giant UV arrow. Captioned `free / cheap · ~90% of the result.` This is the load-bearing element and it must look it — *visibly thicker than everything above it combined.*
  - **Layer 2 — THE 10% (a thin reinforcing membrane on top):** `CORE ACTIVES` — retinoid, vitamin C, moisturizer, exfoliant. A slim warm membrane. Captioned `the 10% that compounds — on top, not instead of.`
  - **Layer 3 — PROCEDURES (bracket-repairs to the scaffold):** red light, microneedling, peels, lasers/RF, drawn as small periodic repair brackets pinned into the dermis. Captioned `force multipliers, not substitutes.`
  - **Layer 4 — ENHANCED TIER (a sliver / specialist patch):** microdose isotretinoin, GHK-Cu, GH — the smallest mark, a tiny specialist patch with a hazard tick. Captioned `last margin · highest risk · smallest delta if 1–3 aren't locked. Rx — bloodwork + physician.`

- **THE COLLAGEN SCAFFOLD (the wall itself, below grade) — the finite substrate.** Beneath the assembly, the dermis is a **lattice/scaffold of collagen** sunk into ground. Where protected, the lattice is dense and upright (warm); where the article's forces reach it unprotected (in the failure ghost, below), it sags, cross-links, and thins. A ground-line caption carries the finite-substrate thesis: `COLLAGEN IS BUILT, NOT BOUGHT — it falls ~1%/yr and the dermal junction flattens with age. You protect the scaffold; you don't manufacture a new one.` Rendered in ghosted graphite where it speaks to the un-creatable base (the same "borrowed foundation" device as Appearance and the "fixed lot" device as Hair).

- **THE REVERSED-ASSEMBLY FAILURE GHOST (the device, reused).** Set apart — a faint, ghosted **second section to the side or behind**, drawn with the order inverted: the expensive interior finish installed (a bright `LASER`/`SERUM` patch) while **the outer protection slab is missing**, so the giant red UV arrow pours straight onto the bare collagen scaffold, which is visibly tearing. One hard caption: `WRONG ORDER — you are paying to repair damage you are still causing.` This is the exact functional twin of Appearance's toppling top-down failure overlay and Hair's condemnation line: a single ghosted counter-state that teaches the order by showing it broken. The contrast between the sound outside-in build and the leaking reversed build *is* the poster's argument made visual.

**The two-register surface (the signature move, reused).** Everything in the **sound assembly is the "protected register"** — crisp blueprint linework, dimensioned layer thicknesses, warm intact scaffold. The **reversed-assembly ghost is the "exposure register"** — washed-out, the scaffold hatched and sagging, the lone red UV arrow unbroken. The surface itself teaches *right-order vs wrong-order* before a word is read.

Why this metaphor fits and nothing else does: the article's literal claims are *an ordered hierarchy* (1→2→3→4), *proportion* (90 vs 10), *direction of attack* (~80% from outside → outside-in is forced), *a ranked set of forces* (four mechanisms, one dominant), and *a finite, un-manufacturable substrate* (collagen −1%/yr). An outside-in wall section, with weather-arrow magnitudes for the forces, layer-thickness for the leverage proportion, a finite scaffold below grade, and a reversed-assembly ghost for the failure mode, is the only object that holds all five at once. A split-face "young vs old" would teach none of them and hide the order; a shelf of serum bottles would make the 10% look like the whole game — the precise error the article exists to refuse.

