## 7. What to Avoid

- **The cliché split-face (young/smooth half vs old/wrinkled half), a dewy "glowing skin" beauty shot, or a woman applying cream.** It says "skincare ad," teaches nothing, and — worse — it hides the *order and proportion*, which is the one idea the article exists to install. Never make a real face the hero. (A small Fitzpatrick I–VI swatch strip may appear *inside* the diagnosis spec card, never as the anchor.)
- **A shelf / flat-lay / pyramid of serum bottles as the hero.** A bottle line-up makes the **10% actives look like the whole game** — the precise wrong-order error ("$90 serum, skip the sunscreen") the article is built to refuse. Products at most appear as tiny dimensioned labels on the thin ACTIVES membrane, never as the central object, and never bigger than the PROTECT slab.
- **(Bonus to avoid:) drawing the four aging forces at equal size, or omitting the reversed-assembly ghost.** Equal-size arrows collapse the ~80% UV insight that forces the outside-in order; dropping the failure ghost removes the "paying to repair damage you're still causing" punchline. The UV arrow must visibly dwarf the other three, and the PROTECT slab must visibly out-thickness everything above it — proportion is the entire poster.
